WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.662 --> 00:00:07.920
[MUSIC]
Corporate social responsibility.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.920
I know CSR doesn't seem like
the sexiest of topics but

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.510
it's actually a pretty huge idea.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:19.070
That business even as it
pursues profit maximization,

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.730
also has a social responsibility.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.310
And I'm here to tell you that
that social responsibility goes

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:30.980
far beyond kind of shiny PR
donations in your hometown.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:36.475
But actually we, as the next
generation of Business Leaders,

00:00:36.475 --> 00:00:40.795
have social responsibilities that are
going to touch the lives of millions of

00:00:40.795 --> 00:00:45.455
people and can even affect some of the
deepest human rights crazies in our world.

00:00:46.995 --> 00:00:52.260
Before the GSB, one of the issues I
focused on was Human Trafficking.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.970
Forced labor in the seafood
industry in Thailand.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.770
Migrant workers being threatened,
denied pay and

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.960
passports, sometimes even killed
onboard Thai fishing boats.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.360
But then something changed.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.780
Because these fishing boats were buried
deep in the supply chains of the frozen

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.210
shrimp we buy at Walmart or Costco.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:21.030
Then these brands were connected in
the headlines to modern day slavery and

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.280
they started paying attention.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.380
I saw this first hand,
sitting around the conference

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.180
room table of the biggest
seafood supplier in Bangkok.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.380
Once we had Walmart and
Costco sitting at that table too,

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.980
insisting on better labor standards,
we started seeing changes in the industry

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:43.630
that hadn't happen over years of
activists, banging on the doors.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:47.230
These companies were addressing
huge supply chain and

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.600
reputational vulnerabilities.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.600
They knew they had to be part
of the responsible solution.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.900
Instead of being held responsible for
the problem.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.390
That's just one example of this
new business imperative and

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:06.040
opportunity to reimagine
responsible business.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:09.760
Three numbers will help me make my case.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.780
They'll tell us the scope
of this opportunity,

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.890
why we should take advantage of it and
what's next.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.090
First, the scope of this opportunity

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.040
of business responsibility is
bigger than you might think.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.968
It can even affect something as seemingly
big and impossible as a distant civil war.

00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:36.570
Because 90% is the share of the world's
rubies that once came from Myanmar.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.470
But Myanmar isn't just famous for
its rubies.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:44.180
It's also famous for
its lingering military dictatorship and

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.510
the wars that the military has
been waging against minorities.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:52.034
Most recently against nearly
1 million Muslims Rohingya.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:57.950
I was working in Myanmar when news about
these atrocities broke this summer

00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.210
researching rubies.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.000
I found that that famous military and

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.470
those famous rubies are more
linked than you might think.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:11.670
Because inside every war isn't
just a central difference or

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.500
disagreement, but also an economy.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:21.560
Because that same Burmese military has its
roots sunk deep into Myanmar's economy.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.450
That means they can self
fund their officers and

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.960
their wars, be insulated from the effect
of things like international sanctions.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:36.770
That means that if we trace back the ruby
from some extra special Valentine's gift,

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:40.620
the odds are pretty decent that
the original mining profits

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.070
went back to the same Burmese military
that's been waging war on its own people.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.265
Now, [LAUGH] I'm not trying to
just be a Valentine's buzz kill.

00:03:50.265 --> 00:03:51.610
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.740
&gt;&gt; Or to say that you not buying a ruby

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.450
can single handedly save the Rohingya.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:01.510
But a company like Tiffany
asking the right questions

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.019
might help separate the Burmese
military from its financial lifelines.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.910
Tiffany has avoided
rubies pretty completely

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.160
because of their responsible
sourcing commitments.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:18.790
But this summer, before the Rohingya
crisis, they were exploring it

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:24.080
because they knew if a company as powerful
as Tiffany could insist on sourcing with

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.780
fair mining standards, transparency and
no military owned mines,

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:34.730
they might help man mars clean ruby
sector have the change to thrive.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:41.030
They could be part of the responsible
solution instead of being responsible for

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:41.560
the problem.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:47.740
Now, [LAUGH] you can still say,
no Mia, I can't afford a ruby ring.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:52.100
And I work on an online tech product,
no conflict affected supply chains.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.790
So stepping closer to home,
what about Facebook?

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:02.588
Fake news hit Ukraine long before
it became a buzz word here.

00:05:02.588 --> 00:05:07.090
Russian government propagandas making
fake Ukrainian Facebook profiles and

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.630
using those to drum up local fears and
tensions.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:15.660
And Ukrainian human rights monitors say
that where this disinformation warfare

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:19.710
was strongest,
actual violence has also been highest.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.140
So can Facebook really get
away with separating its

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:27.480
online platform from the offline
effects it's enabling?

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.328
Unilever just pulled their ad dollars
until Facebook can clean up their

00:05:33.328 --> 00:05:38.095
fake news problem, because they'd
rather be part of the responsible

00:05:38.095 --> 00:05:41.358
solution than be responsible for
the problem.

00:05:41.358 --> 00:05:45.021
Next, $6.3 trillion.

00:05:45.021 --> 00:05:48.361
This is where we get to start
building the business case for

00:05:48.361 --> 00:05:52.587
why good business can be good for
business, why we're going to have to start

00:05:52.587 --> 00:05:56.625
caring about things like the human
rights impact of our work.

00:05:56.625 --> 00:06:01.940
$6.3 trillion is the value of assets
under management at BlockRock,

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.860
the world's largest asset manager.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.820
And their Chairman, Larry Fink,
just wrote a letter to CEOs,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:14.110
putting them on notice that BlockRock is
going to become a more engaged investor.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:20.060
One demanding that its company serve not
only their shareholders, but also society.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:25.600
Vanguard's CEO, with their 4.8 trillion,
is planning to follow suit, and

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.170
this is really my kind of competition.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.700
&gt;&gt; All that means that, like,

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.350
this isn't going to be just
about like watered down CSR,

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:40.489
but about integrating this
into core business functions.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:46.740
So access to investment is one real reason
that a whole variety of human costs

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.960
now need to be built into
your business's bottom line.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.220
I'll quickly give you three more.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.420
First, reputation.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:59.190
It's hard to quantify, [LAUGH] but
we all know it's incredibly important.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:03.189
A single scandal that links your
brand to a human rights abuse,

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:07.807
no matter how far away, it can be
devastating for sales and reputation.

00:07:07.807 --> 00:07:11.559
But on the up side, companies
with values oriented reputations,

00:07:11.559 --> 00:07:15.050
tend to suffer a less of a stock hit,
when problems do come up.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:19.690
Second, there's improved supply chains.

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:23.290
Applying a responsible sourcing lens
means that you can reduce costs,

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.790
by building better supplier
relationships or avoiding disruptions.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.650
Third, millennials like us care
about choosing to work for

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.070
places that are doing good.

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:39.200
As millennials, nearly half of us in this
room say our loyalty to our employer would

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.939
increase if we knew our CEO was
the head of the pack on social issues.

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:51.480
All this means, that if you want to
attract high quality investment, or

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:57.200
earn a resilient reputation, build
responsive supply chains and get the best

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:03.110
talent, you just have to figure out how
to be part of the responsible solution,

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:09.045
not responsible for the problem which
brings me to my last number, one.

00:08:09.045 --> 00:08:14.000
One person, that could be you and

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:20.400
not just you see as our team but you as
you face your next business challenge.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.920
Set standards for
your company and its values.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.990
Measure against them.

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:29.600
Commit to digging into hard
problems instead of walking away.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:34.010
There's no easy playbook for this.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.750
But we now know we have to figure it out.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.650
It's asking who and

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:44.480
where are all the people who's lives
are effected by this business choice?

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.160
From website, to farm,
to factory, to mine.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:52.890
And so the last number is one of hope

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:56.040
because real CSR doesn't
have to be a burden.

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:59.070
It can be the next business opportunity.

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:03.560
It starts with one
responsible person asking one

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:08.360
responsible question, getting on the path
toward being responsible not for

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.886
the problem, but for the next solution.

00:09:10.886 --> 00:09:16.391
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:16.391 --> 00:09:20.350
[MUSIC]

